<title>SAUDI ARABIA: Saudi firm receives 29,000 New Zealand sheep.</title>
<headline>Saudi firm receives 29,000 New Zealand sheep.</headline>
<dateline>DAMMAM, Saudi Arabia 1997-01-13</dateline>
<text>
<p>To meet increased demand during the Islamic month of Ramadan, a Saudi merchant has received a shipment of 29,000 live sheep from New Zealand worth seven million riyals ($1.9 million).</p>
<p>Hamoud Ali al-Khalaf of Hamoud Ali al-Khalaf Trading and Transportation told Reuters on Monday the shipment was part of the 1.2 million live sheep worth some 200 million riyals his company imports annually.</p>
<p>The latest shipment arrived in Dammam in the eastern province late on Saturday after 25 days sailing from New Zealand.</p>
<p>Saudi Arabia is a leading livestock importer and the world's biggest buyer of barley, used to feed camels, sheep and other livestock.</p>
<p>($1=3.75 riyals)</p>
</text>
